// runtime-decimal.metajs -- the SHARED float-to-string rendering, split out of
// runtime.metajs and imported ONLY by the three layer-2 files that use it
// (python, ruby, swift). The exact-decimal bignum underneath it is split again,
// into runtime-bignum.metajs, which only python and ruby import.
//
// WHY IT IS A SEPARATE FILE: a body put into runtime.metajs is paid for by all
// TEN importers, including the seven that cannot call a line of it. Ten files
// import runtime.metajs; three of them want this. So it is imported, not shared.
//
// The ORIGINAL justification here said the cost was the collector walking a
// permanently-live body, and gave "+7-11% wall, +2,304 live bytes x 18,798
// collections". THAT EXPLANATION IS WRONG, re-measured 2026-08-04 in instructions
// retired rather than wall clock (wall clock on this machine varies +-8% between
// runs of the SAME binary; instruction counts reproduce to 0.02%). The collector's
// share does not move at all: with MEC_GC=off subtracted it is 5.09 G instructions
// whether or not the merged bodies are there. The real cost is MUTATOR work in
// runtime.c's `scope_find` (2755), a LINEAR scan of the module scope's name array,
// and it is charged PER TOP-LEVEL DECLARATION - not per byte of live data and not
// per line of code, which is exactly free.
//
// The honest number for THIS file plus runtime-bignum.metajs is 14 declarations
// prepended to a kotlin module: +2.2%, measured (kotlin, 40,000 x `s = s + i % 7`,
// no float in it, 30.506 G -> 31.193 G instructions). Smaller than was claimed,
// still real, and still worth the split. The full derivation, the code-vs-data
// controls and the rule are in docs/runtime-merge-plan.md, "the tax is
// DECLARATIONS, not data and not code".
//
// The MetaJS rules and the rtk* hook convention are runtime.metajs's; this file
// defines one hook of its own, rtkFloDigits, which renders a strictly positive
// finite double the language's own way and is the only part of float-to-string
// that is not shared.

// ============================================ the decimal bignum ===========
//
// THE LARGEST SINGLE DUPLICATION IN LAYER 2, and the extern table could never
// see it. python-rt.metajs and ruby-rt.metajs each carry a complete exact-decimal
// float formatter, reached through pyBFloStr and rbFloStr respectively, and
// swift-rt.metajs carries the splitter as well. No js_* name collides, so the
// 29-name extern census counted these as nine separate private helpers in three
// separate files; a shape scan (normalise every identifier to a positional token,
// then group by the resulting string) puts them in one bucket, 267 lines of it.
//
// python-rt.metajs:2214 SAYS SO at the site - "Copied verbatim from
// languages/lib/ruby-rt.metajs (rbDigitAt / rbBigFromNum / rbBigMulSmall /
// rbBigStr / rbExactDec / rbRoundHalfEven / rbFixed / rbSci / rbBaseStr /
// rbByteCut), renamed to the pyB prefix. The comments are theirs." - which is the
// honest record of a copy and also the reason no gate could have found it.
//
// THE DIFFERENCE LIST, mechanically taken (extract each body, strip the language
// prefix, diff). Ten functions, and the diff is EMPTY for nine of them:
//
//   rtSplit10        py == rb == sw   byte-identical bar swift's three comments
//   rtZeros          py == rb == sw   byte-identical
//   rtDigitAt        py == rb         byte-identical
//   rtBigFromNum     py == rb         byte-identical
//   rtBigMulSmall    py == rb         byte-identical
//   rtBigStr         py == rb         byte-identical
//   rtExactDec       py == rb         byte-identical
//   rtRoundHalfEven  py == rb         byte-identical (57 lines, twice)
//   rtFixed          py == rb         byte-identical
//   rtBaseStr        py == rb         byte-identical bar the name of the digit
//                                     alphabet constant, PYB_DIGITS vs RB_DIGITS,
//                                     whose VALUES are the same 36 characters.
//
// ZERO KNOBS, and that is itself the finding: the two copies could have drifted
// at any point in two separate rounds of formatter work and did not, so nothing
// was hiding here - but nothing was stopping it either, and a fix to one would
// have missed the other in silence. See js_dict_new for the same outcome at
// one-tenth the size.
//
// The float rendering ABOVE this bignum (FloDigits / FloStr) does NOT merge to a
// single body and is handled separately at rtSciText below.

// strconv.FormatFloat(a, 'e', -1, 64), recovered from the engine's own shortest
// rendering: turn a > 0 into {d: the significant digits with no point and no
// leading or trailing zero, e: the SCIENTIFIC exponent}, so that the value is
// 0.d1d2... * 10^(e+1). The input "" + a is either a plain decimal ("1234.5",
// "0.00012") or an exponent form ("1e+21", "1.5e-7"), and both are jsNumString
// in the Go half and d_num_str in the floor, which are the same shortest form
// strconv produces.
function rtSplit10(a) {
    var s = "" + a
    var mant = s
    var e10 = 0
    var ei = s.indexOf("e")
    if (ei >= 0) {
        mant = s.substring(0, ei)
        var es = s.substring(ei + 1, s.length)
        var eneg = false
        if (es.charAt(0) == "+") { es = es.substring(1, es.length) }
        else if (es.charAt(0) == "-") {
            eneg = true
            es = es.substring(1, es.length)
        }
        e10 = parseInt(es)
        if (eneg) { e10 = 0 - e10 }
    }
    // Split the mantissa at its point; `pt` is how many digits precede it.
    var pt = mant.length
    var di = mant.indexOf(".")
    var digits = mant
    if (di >= 0) {
        pt = di
        digits = mant.substring(0, di) + mant.substring(di + 1, mant.length)
    }
    // A leading zero run does not count as significant, and each one shifts the
    // point right by one place.
    var lead = 0
    while (lead < digits.length && digits.charAt(lead) == "0") { lead = lead + 1 }
    digits = digits.substring(lead, digits.length)
    pt = pt - lead
    // A trailing zero run is not significant either, and does not move the point.
    var end = digits.length
    while (end > 1 && digits.charAt(end - 1) == "0") { end = end - 1 }
    digits = digits.substring(0, end)
    if (digits.length == 0) { return {d: "0", e: 0} }
    return {d: digits, e: e10 + pt - 1}
}

// rtZeros MOVED to lib/runtime.metajs (fifth pass): php had the same nine lines
// as phZeros and has no decimal bignum to import this file for.

// The exponential half of a float rendering, shared by python, ruby and swift:
// one digit, the rest as a fraction, then a SIGNED exponent zero-padded to two
// digits. `dot` forces a fraction digit onto a one-digit mantissa, which is
// Ruby's rule (1.0e+20) and not Python's or Swift's (1e+20).
//
// This, and not the whole of FloDigits, is what the three languages share. The
// BOUNDARY between exponential and plain form is three genuinely different rules
// and stays in the three files: Python's is `decpt <= -4 || decpt > 16`, Ruby's
// is numeric.c flo_to_s's `decpt < -3 || decpt > DBL_DIG(15)`, and Swift's is on
// the VALUE rather than the exponent - SwiftDtoa goes scientific above 2^53, so
// 9007199254740992.0 is the last plain one. The two low bounds are the SAME
// predicate spelled twice (decpt <= -4 and decpt < -3 both mean e10 <= -5); only
// the high bound really differs, and it differs three ways.
function rtSciText(digits, e10, dot) {
    var out = digits.substring(0, 1)
    if (digits.length > 1) { out = out + "." + digits.substring(1, digits.length) }
    else if (dot) { out = out + ".0" }
    var sign = "+"
    var ex = e10
    if (ex < 0) {
        sign = "-"
        ex = 0 - ex
    }
    if (ex < 10) { return out + "e" + sign + "0" + ex }
    return out + "e" + sign + ex
}

// The plain half, with `decpt` digits before the point and a forced ".0" when
// there is no fraction left. Byte-identical in all three, down to the spelling.
function rtFixText(digits, decpt) {
    if (decpt <= 0) { return "0." + rtZeros(0 - decpt) + digits }
    if (decpt >= digits.length) { return digits + rtZeros(decpt - digits.length) + ".0" }
    return digits.substring(0, decpt) + "." + digits.substring(decpt, digits.length)
}

// The whole of a language's float-to-string, above rtkFloDigits (which renders a
// strictly positive finite double and is the one part that differs). python and
// ruby had this body twice and swift a third time; the DIFFERENCE LIST is three
// string literals and nothing else:
//
//   nan / inf / -inf        python (repr(float('nan')) is "nan"), swift
//   NaN / Infinity / -Inf…  ruby (Float::NAN.to_s is "NaN", INFINITY "Infinity")
//
// so they are parameters rather than hooks. KNOB, and a real one: it is what the
// language prints, not how it computes. Everything else agreed, including the
// two spellings of the sign test - python's `x < 0` after the zero arm and
// swift's swSignBit + unary minus reach the same two answers, because the zero
// arm has already taken every value whose sign a comparison cannot see.
//
// -0.0 keeps its sign, which needs 1 / x: `-0.0 < 0` is false, Math.abs of it is
// +0.0, and `"" + -0.0` is "0" in every engine here.
function rtFloStr(x, nanS, infS, ninfS) {
    if (x !== x) { return nanS }
    // The infinities without naming the Infinity global, which the interpreter
    // half spells 1 / 0 too (languages/metajs-interpreter.abnf): Inf - Inf is NaN.
    if (x - x !== 0) {
        if (x > 0) { return infS }
        return ninfS
    }
    if (x == 0) {
        if (1 / x < 0) { return "-0.0" }
        return "0.0"
    }
    if (x < 0) { return "-" + rtkFloDigits(0 - x) }
    return rtkFloDigits(x)
}
